sec.p:
The Guangdong–Hong Kong–Macao Greater Bay Area combines nine cities in Guangdong Province with Hong Kong and Macao within a coastal airshed and a heterogeneous urban economy. It therefore presents both an environmental management problem and a coordination problem across jurisdictions. Studies of China’s clean air actions document strong particulate reductions and a more complicated ozone (O




        3



) response [19], [20]. Regional work also identifies consequences for health and household expenditure [21], [22]. The value of a contemporary monitoring assessment lies in showing whether recent progress has been shared across the constituent cities and whether the remaining pattern calls for the same response for every pollutant.

Previous studies provide a basis for considering coordinated management of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) and nitrogen oxides (NO



      x


), together with the timing and spatial coverage of controls [32], [33], [34], [35], [36], [37]. The monitoring results presented here do not determine an optimal precursor reduction ratio. That decision requires chemical sensitivity analysis and emissions information. They do, however, show why reducing one pollutant cannot be taken as evidence that the broader urban air quality problem has been resolved. Further economic upgrading, transport electrification or fuel substitution should be assessed against its pollutant-specific outcomes and the relevant regional electricity and supply chains, rather than assumed to improve every ambient indicator equally.
